---
import DocLink from '@/components/DocLink.astro';
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Choices from '@/components/motion/Choices.astro';
import Choice from '@/components/motion/Choice.astro';
import Steps from '@/components/motion/Steps.astro';
import Step from '@/components/motion/Step.astro';
import GoodToKnow from '@/components/motion/GoodToKnow.astro';
import Fact from '@/components/motion/Fact.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import HtmlExample from '@/components/motion/HtmlExample.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import { MotionTextReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-text.tsx';
import { MotionTextAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-text.ts';
import MotionTextSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-text.svelte';
import { glossaryHref } from '@/lib/motion-glossary';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="text"
  title="Motion: animate text"
  description="Split a text into words, characters or lines, and play each piece in turn, while a screen reader reads the text once. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    Each word, letter or line of a sentence plays in turn, and a screen reader reads the sentence once, as written.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="when-to-use-it">When to use it</h2>
  <Choices size="compact">
    <Choice lead href="#how-it-behaves" link="How it behaves">
      <Fragment slot="title">split: 'words'</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="when">
        a sentence or a title should arrive piece by piece: each word, letter or line plays in turn as the text enters the view.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">
        the sentence is read once, as written: the <DocLink href={glossaryHref('split')}>split</DocLink> is a visual copy, hidden from assistive
        technology.
      </Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice when="Prefer it when" href="/motion/reveal" link="Reveal">
      <Fragment slot="title">verb: 'reveal'</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="when">a block enters the view as one piece: a card, an image, a row.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">it has no pieces, so nothing is split or copied.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice when="Prefer it when" code={false} title="Counters" href="/motion/values#counters" link="Counters">
      <Fragment slot="when">a number changes, and counts to each new value.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">it is a value with a format, and a screen reader gets the final number.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
    <Choice when="Prefer it when" code={false} title="Sequences" href="/motion/sequences" link="Sequences">
      <Fragment slot="when">several elements play in order, from one root: a title, then a subtitle, then a button.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="because">one handle plays, pauses and seeks the steps.</Fragment>
    </Choice>
  </Choices>

  <h2 id="play-a-lyric-word-by-word">Play a lyric, word by word</h2>
  <p>
    A lyric of the player, one paragraph per line. Its words play in turn when the line enters the view, and each press of Next
    line shows the next one, whose words play again: a new state plays the reveal, whatever the view.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      Put <code>data-motion="headline"</code> on each line, call <code>connectMotion()</code> once, and set
      <code>data-motion-state</code> to whether the line is the current one.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">Render each line as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> with <code>state</code> set to whether it is the current one.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Put <code>udxMotion</code> on each line, and bind <code>[udxMotionState]</code> to whether it is the current one.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Attach <code>motion()</code> to each line, and pass whether it is the current one as its <code>state</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  {/* Hydrated when idle, below the fold: the entry plays as the demo enters the view. */}
  <MotionUsage name="motion-text" layout="split" previewHeight={[204, 284]}>
    <HtmlExample slot="html" name="motion-text" />
    <MotionTextReact slot="react" client:idle />
    <MotionTextAngular slot="angular" client:idle />
    <MotionTextSvelte slot="svelte" client:idle />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2 id="how-it-behaves">How it behaves</h2>
  <h3 id="what-starts-it">What starts it</h3>
  <p>
    The <DocLink href={glossaryHref('trigger')}>trigger</DocLink> is the sentence entering the view, or a new state: then the words play in
    turn. A sentence that is already in view when the page loads keeps its natural state, as any reveal does, so nothing plays until
    its state changes. See <DocLink href="/motion/troubleshooting#nothing-animates">Troubleshooting</DocLink>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="the-words-in-turn">The words, in turn</h3>
  <Steps label="The life of a split">
    <Step title="Split">The runtime cuts the text into words, letters or lines, in reading order.</Step>
    <Step title="Waiting">Each piece waits in its first frame, until the sentence enters the view.</Step>
    <Step title="Playing">The pieces play in turn, 20 ms apart unless the definition sets a <DocLink href={glossaryHref('stagger')}>stagger</DocLink>.</Step>
    <Step title="At rest">Each piece lets go of its inline styles: the sentence is in place (<DocLink href={glossaryHref('at rest')}>at rest</DocLink>).</Step>
  </Steps>
  <p>
    The original nodes move into a visually hidden copy, which keeps their semantics: <code>em</code>, <code>strong</code> and
    <code>abbr</code> stay as they are. The copy the eye sees is <code>aria-hidden</code>, so a screen reader reads the sentence
    once, as written. A change of the text is cut again.
  </p>

  <h3 id="rules">Rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A new state plays the words again, whatever the view: that is how the lyric moves on.</li>
    <li>A text with a link, a button, a field or a <code>tabindex</code> is not split: it plays whole.</li>
    <li><code>split</code> is an option of a reveal or an appear, not a <DocLink href={glossaryHref('verb')}>verb</DocLink>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="variants">Variants</h2>
  <h3 id="replay-the-sentence">Replay the sentence</h3>
  <p>A new state is the simplest way to play the sentence again: see the lyric above. To replay it from code, call <code>play()</code> on its binding.</p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Call <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>: the reveal plays again from its first frame.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass a <code>motionRef</code> to the <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>, and call <code>play()</code> on it: the reveal plays again from its first frame.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Take the directive in a template reference, <code>#lyric="udxMotion"</code>, and call its <code>play()</code>.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Take the element with <code>bind:this</code>, and call <code>getMotion(element, 'view')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h3 id="split-by-letters-or-lines">Split by letters or by lines</h3>
  <p>
    Set <code>split</code> to <code>chars</code> for the letters, as graphemes, or to <code>lines</code> for the lines of the text.
    With <code>lines</code>, the words that share a line play together, and a resize rebuilds the lines once the motion in progress
    has ended.
  </p>

  <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
  <p>
    <code>split</code> is one of the options of a reveal, or of an appear. <code>stagger</code> and <code>origin</code> set the
    cascade of its pieces.
  </p>
  <MotionOptions verb="reveal" omit={['repeat', 'axis']} />

  <h2 id="good-to-know">Good to know</h2>
  <GoodToKnow>
    <Fact label="Reduced motion">
      <p>
        Under reduced motion (the <DocLink href={glossaryHref('policy')}>policy</DocLink>), the text is not split: the element plays whole, and the
        lyric fades in place (<code>reduced: 'fade'</code>). With <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor, the
        sentence shows at once, without a split.
      </p>
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Timing">
      <p>
        Each piece moves on the default spatial spring and fades on the default effects spring, as an entry does. The pieces cascade
        tight, 20 ms apart, unless the definition sets a stagger.
      </p>
      <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects', 'stagger.tight']} />
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Limits">
      <ul>
        <li>
          If the element holds a link, a button, a field or an element with a <code>tabindex</code>, it is not split: it plays whole,
          and a development build says why. The same goes for a text that changes: if the new text holds one of them, the split is
          undone and the text is given back whole, as written. Put the motion on a text element that holds none of them.
        </li>
        <li>
          If you split by lines and the text holds an element other than a line break, the lines fall back to words. Remove that
          element, or split by words.
        </li>
        <ForFramework as="li">
          <Fragment slot="react">If React renders elements inside a split text, keep the split off that text: the split moves those nodes into a span of its own, so React no longer finds them under its element.</Fragment>
          <Fragment slot="angular">If Angular renders elements inside a split text, keep the split off that text: the split moves those nodes into a span of its own, so Angular no longer finds them under its element.</Fragment>
          <Fragment slot="svelte">If Svelte renders elements inside a split text, keep the split off that text: the split moves those nodes into a span of its own, so Svelte no longer finds them under its element.</Fragment>
        </ForFramework>
        <li>Something else? See <DocLink href="/motion/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</DocLink> and <DocLink href="/motion/limits">Limits</DocLink>.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fact>
  </GoodToKnow>
</MotionPage>
